Background. A vertex operator algebra (VOA) is a Z-graded vector space V = ⊕_{n∈Z} V_{(n)} (with dim V_{(n)} < ∞ and V_{(n)} = 0 for n sufficiently negative) equipped with a vacuum vector 1, a conformal vector ω, and a vertex operator map Y(·,x): V → (End V)[[x,x^{-1}]] satisfying the standard axioms (vacuum, creation, Jacobi identity / weak commutativity and associativity, Virasoro relations coming from Y(ω,x)). A meromorphic open-string vertex algebra (MOSVA) is a noncommutative generalization of a VOA: it is a Z-graded vector space with grading-restriction conditions, a vacuum, a conformal vector and a vertex operator map Y(·,x) satisfying the vacuum property, the d/dx-derivative property, the Virasoro relations, and a meromorphic associativity axiom, but not requiring the (skew-)symmetry that holds for a VOA. Every VOA V is in particular a MOSVA. A V-bimodule W for V regarded as a MOSVA is a graded vector space equipped with a left and a right vertex operator action of V which together satisfy meromorphic associativity. 

For V regarded as a MOSVA and for a V-bimodule W there is a cohomology theory H^n(V, W) (n ∈ Z_+) defined analogously to Hochschild cohomology of associative algebras, constructed from cochains given by certain rational-function-valued multilinear maps that are required to satisfy suitable convergence and compatibility (composability) conditions.

Problem. Let V be a vertex operator algebra of one of the following types:
(a) a Wess–Zumino–Witten model VOA L(k,0) associated to an affine Kac–Moody algebra at positive integer level;
(b) a minimal model VOA (the simple Virasoro VOA L(c_{p,q},0) at a discrete-series central charge);
(c) a lattice VOA V_L associated to a positive-definite even lattice L;
(d) the moonshine module vertex operator algebra V^♮.

Prove or disprove: for every such V, every V-bimodule W (where V is viewed as a meromorphic open-string vertex algebra), and every positive integer n, the cohomology group H^n(V, W) vanishes.

